## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to model simple domain data, perform file-based persistence with Codable, and drive UI with SwiftUI while handling dates and basic filtering.

## Key Concepts
- SwiftUI List and navigation
- Codable JSON persistence to Documents
- File I/O and error handling
- Date arithmetic for overdue
- Lightweight test scaffolding

## Code Example
```swift
struct Task: Codable {
  let id: UUID
  var title: String
  var dueDate: Date
  var isDone: Bool
}

final class TaskStore {
  private var tasks: [Task] = []
  private let url: URL
  init(filename: String = "tasks.json") {
    let dir = FileManager.default.urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask).first!
    self.url = dir.appendingPathComponent(filename)
    load()
  }
  func load() {
    if let data = try? Data(contentsOf: url), let decoded = try? JSONDecoder().decode([Task].self, from: data) {
      tasks = decoded
    }
  }
  func save() {
    if let data = try? JSONEncoder().encode(tasks) {
      try? data.write(to: url)
    }
  }
  // add/edit/delete helpers...
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you migrate to Core Data if the feature set grows? 
- How would you detect and recover from partial write failures?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a robust, real-time data layer that stays responsive under network variability, using concurrency primitives and local caching.

## Key Concepts

- Swift concurrency with async/await and TaskGroups
- Local cache using SQLite
- Delta-based merge with per-item versioning
- Conflict resolution and ordering guarantees
- Test strategy for latency, reordering, and memory pressure

## Code Example

```swift
struct Post { let id: String; var version: Int; var content: String }
struct Delta { let id: String; let version: Int; let content: String }

func merge(_ deltas: [Delta], into cache: inout [String: Post]) -> [Post] {
  for d in deltas {
    if var existing = cache[d.id] {
      if d.version > existing.version {
        existing.version = d.version
        existing.content = d.content
        cache[d.id] = existing
      }
    } else {
      cache[d.id] = Post(id: d.id, version: d.version, content: d.content)
    }
  }
  return Array(cache.values)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and mitigate race conditions in the delta merge?
- How would you extend to multi-device sync and server-driven conflict resolution?

## Why This Is Asked
